# elasticMS links and file references

## Purpose

Use this guide when you create or update elasticMS documents through MCP.

elasticMS uses different link formats depending on the field type:

- data links for references to other CMS documents;
- asset objects for file fields;
- internal `ems://` links inside WYSIWYG HTML.

Use the exact format expected by the target field.

## Data link fields

Data links are used by fields based on:

`EMS\CoreBundle\Form\DataField\DataLinkFieldType`

A single data link is a string with this format:

```text
{content_type_name}:{ouuid}
```

Example:

```text
news:7b3d9a4f-6a2f-4f9d-8b5a-2df5f0b4c123
```

`content_type_name` is the technical name of the linked content type.

`ouuid` is the object unique identifier of the linked document.

When a `DataLinkFieldType` field is multiple, the value is an array of data link
strings:

```json
[
    "news:7b3d9a4f-6a2f-4f9d-8b5a-2df5f0b4c123",
    "event:1d3e8a62-ec40-4d2f-91bb-39ad7c7a4567"
]
```

## File fields

File links are used by fields based on:

- `EMS\CoreBundle\Form\DataField\AssetFieldType`
- `EMS\CoreBundle\Form\DataField\IndexedAssetFieldType`

The field value is an object with file metadata:

```json
{
    "sha1": "9a323a5b625999e1a13ed88e0b14c86cf0e76edb",
    "mimetype": "application/pdf",
    "filesize": 123456,
    "filename": "document.pdf",
    "_algo": "sha1"
}
```

Before writing this object into a document, upload the file itself with the MCP
asset tools:

1. Call `init_asset_upload`.
2. Send the file bytes with one or more `upload_asset_chunk` calls.
3. Use the returned `sha1`, `mimetype`, `filesize`, `_algo`, and `filename` values in
   the field.

Do not invent the `sha1`, `filesize` or `_algo`. They must describe the uploaded file content.

Fields based on `IndexedAssetFieldType` can also contain these optional
attributes:

- `_author`: file author;
- `_title`: file title;
- `_date`: file date;
- `_language`: file language;
- `_content`: file summary used to feed the search engine.

Provide these optional attributes when they are available. The `_content` name
is misleading: it is expected to contain a summary, not the full file content.
Before generating this summary, ask the user whether they want the agent to
summarize the file.

## WYSIWYG fields

WYSIWYG links are used inside HTML stored by fields based on:

`EMS\CoreBundle\Form\DataField\WysiwygFieldType`

Internal WYSIWYG links use the `ems://` URI scheme.

The class that parses these links is:

`EMS\CommonBundle\Common\EMSLink`

## Links to CMS documents in WYSIWYG

Use this format for an internal link to a CMS document:

```text
ems://object:{content_type_name}:{ouuid}
```

Example:

```html
<a href="ems://object:news:7b3d9a4f-6a2f-4f9d-8b5a-2df5f0b4c123">
    Read the news
</a>
```

`content_type_name` is the technical name of the linked content type.

`ouuid` is the object unique identifier of the linked document.

## Links to assets in WYSIWYG

Use this format for an internal link to an uploaded asset:

```text
ems://asset:{sha1}?type={mime_type}&name={filename}
```

Example:

```html
<a href="ems://asset:9a323a5b625999e1a13ed88e0b14c86cf0e76edb?type=application/pdf&name=document.pdf">
    Download the document
</a>
```

The query attributes are:

- `type`: the MIME type of the file;
- `name`: the filename served to the user.

The `type` and `name` query attributes are optional.

If `type` is missing, the asset is served with:

```text
application.bin
```

If `name` is missing, the asset is served with:

```text
filename.bin
```

For a good user experience, always provide both `type` and `name` when
generating WYSIWYG asset links.

## Recommended workflow for files used in WYSIWYG

1. Upload the file with `init_asset_upload` and `upload_asset_chunk`.
2. Read the returned file metadata.
3. Build the `ems://asset:{sha1}` link.
4. Add the `type` query parameter with the MIME type.
5. Add the `name` query parameter with the filename.
6. Insert the link into the WYSIWYG HTML.

Example:

```html
<p>
    <a href="ems://asset:9a323a5b625999e1a13ed88e0b14c86cf0e76edb?type=application/pdf&name=brochure.pdf">
        Download the brochure
    </a>
</p>
```

## Quick reference

- Single document reference:
  - field type: `DataLinkFieldType`
  - value: `"{content_type_name}:{ouuid}"`
- Multiple document references:
  - field type: `DataLinkFieldType`
  - value: array of `"{content_type_name}:{ouuid}"` strings
- File field:
  - field type: `AssetFieldType` or `IndexedAssetFieldType`
  - value: object with `sha1`, `mimetype`, `filesize`, and `filename`
- WYSIWYG document link:
  - field type: `WysiwygFieldType`
  - value: `ems://object:{content_type_name}:{ouuid}`
- WYSIWYG asset link:
  - field type: `WysiwygFieldType`
  - value: `ems://asset:{sha1}?type={mime_type}&name={filename}`

## Validation checklist

Before saving a document, verify that:

- every data link uses an existing content type name and document OUUID;
- every multiple data link field is an array;
- every file field uses metadata returned by the MCP asset upload tools;
- every WYSIWYG document link starts with `ems://object:`;
- every WYSIWYG asset link starts with `ems://asset:`;
- WYSIWYG asset links include `type` and `name` whenever possible.
